Mathematical References
-----------------------

The shot angle geometry calculation (dbt_project/macros/shot_angle.sql)
references "The Geometry of Shooting" from Soccermatics by David Sumpter.
The implementation is an independent SQL translation of a well-known
trigonometric formula, not a derivative of any source code.

The physics-based pitch control model (src/analytics/pitch_control.py)
implements the time-to-intercept and influence model described in:
Spearman, W. (2017). "Beyond Expected Goals." MIT Sloan Sports
Analytics Conference. The implementation is an independent Python
translation of the published equations, not derived from any source code.

The VAEP action valuation pipeline (src/ingestion/spadl_vaep.py) uses the
SPADL representation and VAEP framework described in: Decroos, T. et al.
(2019). "Actions Speak Louder than Goals: Valuing Player Actions in
Soccer." Proceedings of the 25th ACM SIGKDD. Implementation via the
socceraction library (see Third-Party Libraries above).

The line-breaking pass detection algorithm (src/analytics/line_breaking.py)
is adapted from: parmacalcio1913/line-breaking-passes, licensed under the
Apache License, Version 2.0. The implementation is rewritten from PyTorch
to NumPy, adapted for StatsBomb 120x80 coordinates, and extended with
sideline extensions and batch processing.
See: https://github.com/parmacalcio1913/line-breaking-passes

The Expected Threat (xT) grid seed values were originally based on the
12x8 zone probability values from: Karun Singh (2018), "Introducing
Expected Threat (xT)." The grid has since been replaced by a data-driven
xT grid computed from 2.2M SPADL actions (stored in the
expected_threat_grids Delta table). Attribution is professional courtesy.
See: https://karun.in/blog/expected-threat.html

The Off-Ball Expected Threat module (src/analytics/off_ball_xt.py) combines
pitch control (Spearman 2017, credited above) with xT zones to quantify
off-ball positional value. The concept follows Soccermatics Lesson 7 by
David Sumpter. The implementation is an independent Python translation,
not derived from any source code.

The DEFCON-lite defensive credit assignment (src/analytics/defcon_lite.py)
implements the heuristic credit assignment framework and defensive action
taxonomy described in: Kim, H.S. et al. (2025). "Better Prevent than
Tackle: Valuing Defense in Soccer Based on Graph Neural Networks."
arXiv:2512.10355. The implementation is an independent Python translation
of the paper's Tier 1-3 equations (intercept/concede/disturb/deter
classification, spatial features, XGBoost confidence), not derived from
the authors' source code. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
See: https://github.com/hyunsungkim-ds/defcon

The player embedding pipeline (src/analytics/football2vec.py) implements
the spatial tokenization and Doc2Vec embedding approach described in:
Theiner, J. et al. (2022). "Exploring the Relationship Between Football
Players' Positions and Their Attributes through Network Analysis" (also
known as "Football2Vec"). The implementation is an independent Python
translation using gensim Doc2Vec. Model weights are published to
HuggingFace Hub (luxury-lakehouse/football2vec-statsbomb-wyscout).
Reference implementation: https://github.com/ofirmg/football2vec (MIT).

The cross-source player entity resolution pipeline
(src/analytics/entity_resolution.py) uses a three-layer progressive
matching strategy inspired by: USSoccerFederation/glass_onion, licensed
under the BSD 3-Clause License. Copyright (c) 2024 U.S. Soccer Federation.
The multi-layer progressive strategy, DOB month/day swap detection, and
jersey-as-constraint patterns are adapted from the glass_onion design.
The implementation is an independent Python translation using TF-IDF
character n-grams (sparse_dot_topn) and rapidfuzz multi-attribute scoring,
not derived from the authors' source code.
See: https://github.com/USSoccerFederation/glass_onion

The ELASTIC event-tracking synchronization algorithm
(src/analytics/elastic_sync.py) implements the feature-based alignment
framework described in: Kim, H.S. et al. (2025). "ELASTIC: Event-Tracking
Data Synchronization in Soccer Without Annotated Event Locations."
ECML-PKDD MLSA 2025. arXiv:2508.09238. The implementation is an
independent Python translation of the published equations. Licensed under
the Apache License, Version 2.0.
See: https://github.com/hyunsungkim-ds/defcon

The PAUSA pass timing pipeline (src/ingestion/pausa.py) and OBSO value
surface computation (src/analytics/obso.py) implement the optimal pass
timing decomposition described in: Lee, Jo, Hong, Bauer & Ko (2026).
"Valuing La Pausa: Quantifying Optimal Pass Timing Beyond Speed."
MIT Sloan Sports Analytics Conference 2026. Licensed under the
Apache License, Version 2.0.
See: https://github.com/leemingo/mitssac-pausa

The OBSO (Off-Ball Scoring Opportunity) value surface concept combines
pitch control, ball transition probability, and expected possession value
as described in: Spearman, W. (2018). "Beyond Expected Goals." MIT Sloan
Sports Analytics Conference; and Fernandez, J. & Bornn, L. (2018).
"Wide Open Spaces: A statistical technique for measuring space creation
in professional soccer." MIT Sloan Sports Analytics Conference. These are
independent implementations of published academic concepts.

The physics-based tracking augmentation module
(src/analytics/augmentation.py) applies positional jitter techniques
inspired by: Wang, Z. et al. (2024). "TacticAI: an AI assistant for
football tactics." Nature Communications 15, 1906. The implementation
is an independent Python translation of augmentation principles, not
derived from the TacticAI source code.

Space Creation Quantification
Fernandez, J. & Bornn, L. (2018). "Wide Open Spaces: A statistical technique
for measuring space creation in professional soccer." MIT Sloan Sports Analytics Conference.

